message.content:
Below is a minimal example that shows how you would invoke the helper function `PlainTextPresentation_createPresentation` to wire up a **read‑only, multi‑line** text area that automatically gets horizontal and vertical scroll bars.  
The function itself takes two *string* arguments that represent the Java objects you want to pass in – in this case the `IResultSetController` instance (`dataController`) and the SWT `Composite` that will host the view (`compositeParent`).  

```java
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1.  Prepare the Java objects that you already have in your UI
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
IResultSetController dataController = ...;   // your controller instance
Composite compositeParent = ...;            // the parent composite

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2.  Call the helper that creates the plain‑text presentation
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
Object result = functions.PlainTextPresentation_createPresentation(
    new HashMap<String, Object>() {{
        put("controller", dataController);   // <-- the controller instance
        put("parent",     compositeParent);  // <-- the parent composite
    }}
);

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3.  (Optional) Verify that the returned object is what you expect
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
if (result instanceof StyledText) {
    StyledText styledText = (StyledText) result;
    // The helper already sets:
    //   - styledText.setEditable(false);          // read‑only
    //   - styledText.setMultiLine(true);          // multi‑line
    //   - styledText.setScrollBars(SWT.V_SCROLL | SWT.H_SCROLL);
    // If you need to tweak anything else you can do it here.
}
```

### What the helper does under the hood

| Feature | How it’s achieved |
|---------|-------------------|
| **Read‑only** | `styledText.setEditable(false)` |
| **Multi‑line** | `styledText.setMultiLine(true)` |
| **Horizontal & vertical scrolling** | `styledText.setScrollBars(SWT.V_SCROLL | SWT.H_SCROLL)` |
| **Styling** | The helper typically creates a `StyledText` widget, attaches a `StyledTextPresentation` (or similar) that listens to the controller’s result set changes, and updates the text content accordingly. |

> **Tip:** If you need to add additional listeners (e.g., to react to user selections or to format the text differently), you can do so after the helper returns the `StyledText` instance, as shown in step 3 above.

That’s all you need to get a fully functional, read‑only, multi‑line, scrollable plain‑text presentation for your `dataController`.

